# install-macos.sh is served to macOS users and run by bash. A CRLF checkout
# committed back from a Windows machine would break it in a way that is hard to
# read: bash reports `$'\r': command not found` on the first line and every
# line after it, and the script looks corrupt rather than mis-encoded.
# core.autocrlf=true on the author's machine makes that one careless commit away,
# so pin it rather than rely on the blob happening to be clean.
*.sh text eol=lf
